.TH TOOLCHEST 1 "October 2026" "emwm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
toolchest \- a menu of programs, after the IRIX Interactive Desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B toolchest
[\fIX options\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fImenufile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B toolchest
shows a small window with a column of buttons, each opening a menu of
programs and desktop actions. It is meant to run alongside
.BR emwm (1).
.PP
The menus are read from
.BR ~/.toolchestrc ,
or, if that does not exist, from the system file
.BR system.chestrc .
They use the menu syntax of
.BR emwmrc (4):
.PP
.nf
    Menu ToolChest
    {
        "Desktop"            f.menu Desktop
    }

    Menu Desktop
    {
        "Open Unix Shell"  _O  f.exec "xterm -ls"
        no-label               f.separator
        "Log Out"          _L  f.logout
    }
.fi
.PP
The menu named
.B ToolChest
is the column of buttons. Each entry is a label, an optional mnemonic
(\fB_\fIx\fR) and a function:
.TP
.BI f.exec " command"
Run \fIcommand\fR with /bin/sh.
.TP
.BI f.menu " name"
Open the menu \fIname\fR.
.TP
.B f.separator
A line between entries.
.TP
.B f.title
A label that cannot be selected.
.TP
.B f.logout
Ask, then end the session by sending SIGTERM to the process in
.BR DESKTOP_SESSION_PID .
A session script sets that to its own pid and then execs the window
manager, so logging out stops the window manager and ends the session.
.TP
.B f.restart
Read the menus again.
.TP
.B f.quit
Close the Toolchest.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " menufile"
Read the menus from \fImenufile\fR.
.SH RESOURCES
The application class is
.BR Toolchest .
The column of buttons is the widget
.BR chest .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.toolchestrc
Your menus.
.TP
.B system.chestrc
The system's menus, in the X11 library directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR emwm (1),
.BR emwmrc (4)
